.TH SHARDPOSTCTL-MAILBOX 1 "2026-10-03" "ShardPost 0.51.0" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpostctl-mailbox \- list, inspect, create, rename and subscribe mailboxes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpostctl mailbox list
.RB [ \-all ]
.RB [ \-limit
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-after
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox show
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account mailbox
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox create
.RB [ \-special-use
.IR list ]
.I account name
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox rename
.RB [ \-if-match
.IR etag ]
.I account mailbox "new name"
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox subscribe
.RB [ \-if-match
.IR etag ]
.I account mailbox
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox unsubscribe
.RB [ \-if-match
.IR etag ]
.I account mailbox
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox lock status
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox lock enable \-pods
.I n
.RB [ \-force ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B shardpostctl mailbox lock disable
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.I mailbox
argument is either the mailbox name as the client sees it
.RI ( INBOX ", " Archive/2026 )
or its stable id. The client resolves a name to its id first, so a
mailbox that is renamed between two commands is still the same object.
.SS mailbox list
Mailboxes in name order with id, UIDVALIDITY, UIDNEXT, HIGHESTMODSEQ,
subscription, special-use and the number of ACL entries. One page holds
.B \-limit
entries (default 100);
.B \-after
continues after a name, and
.B \-all
fetches every page. This command reads the registry and one header per
mailbox, so it stays cheap even for large accounts.
.PP
Role:
.BR diagnose .
.SS mailbox show
One mailbox in detail. On top of the listing it reports the message
count, how many are unseen and flagged deleted, the total size, the
number of index shards and their size, where the change log starts (the
QRESYNC horizon) and the keywords in use. It reads every index shard of
the mailbox, so it costs more than
.BR "mailbox list" .
.PP
The shard line is worth knowing:
.I "shards 47 of 20 UIDs"
means the mailbox was created when
.I mbox_shard_size
was 20. The size is fixed when the mailbox is created and never changes
afterwards; only a rebuild (export, purge, restore) moves an existing
mailbox to the current size.
.PP
Role:
.BR diagnose .
.SS mailbox create
Creates a mailbox, with parents as needed.
.B \-special-use
takes a comma-separated list
.RI ( \e\fBArchive\fP ", " \e\fBSent\fP ", " \e\fBJunk\fP ", " \e\fBTrash\fP ", "
.IR \e\fBDrafts\fP ", " \e\fBAll\fP ", " \e\fBFlagged\fP ", " \e\fBImportant\fP );
it is stored in the usual spelling. If the mailbox already exists with
the same special-use, the command succeeds and changes nothing, so a
repeated run after a lost answer is safe. With a different special-use it
is a conflict.
.PP
A new name must be valid UTF-8 without control characters, without
.BR * ", " % " and " \e ,
without bidirectional controls, invisible characters such as a zero
width space or a byte order mark, or Unicode noncharacters; no level may
be empty,
.BR . " or " .. ,
start or end with white space, or lack a visible character; a level is
at most 255 bytes and the name at most 1024. A name below the shared or
public namespace prefix is refused as well. Levels that already exist
are not checked, so an existing mailbox is answered as such and a
mailbox can be created below an old name the rules would refuse. The
rules and their reasons are in the section \(lqMailbox names\(rq of
.IR docs/protocols.md .
A name that breaks them is refused with exit status 64 and the reason.
.PP
Role:
.BR provision .
.SS mailbox rename
Renames a mailbox together with its children. INBOX cannot be renamed,
and an existing target is a conflict. Renaming to the current name does
nothing. The new name follows the rules of
.BR "mailbox create" ,
and no mailbox moved along may end up longer than 1024 bytes; a mailbox
whose old name breaks the rules can still be renamed to a valid one.
.PP
Role:
.BR provision .
.SS mailbox subscribe, mailbox unsubscribe
Sets or clears the subscription flag that clients use to decide which
mailboxes to show.
.PP
Role:
.BR provision .
.SS mailbox lock status
Shows whether the cluster mark of the mailbox lock
.RB ( mailbox/lock ,
since 0.52.0) is set, with the server registrations as
.B fts layout3 status
shows them. The mark allows the
.B mailbox_delete
job (see
.BR shardpostctl-job (1))
to lock only the mailboxes it removes instead of the whole account:
deliveries, logins and sessions go on, a write into a locked mailbox is
refused, a Sieve
.B fileinto
into one goes to INBOX, and only a session that has one selected is
closed. A server before 0.52.0 does not see the lock and would deliver
into a mailbox that is being deleted, so the mark needs every server to
know it. Without the mark the job takes the account lock, as before
0.52.0. Role:
.BR diagnose .
.SS mailbox lock enable
Sets the mark when exactly
.I n
fresh registrations of servers report the capability
.BR mailbox_lock ;
otherwise it is refused with exit code 65 naming the instances.
.B \-force
sets it without the check (audited). Plans with
.B \-online
are allowed from then on, and with
.I "jobs.mailbox_delete_online: auto"
also plans without it. Role:
.BR repair .
.SS mailbox lock disable
Removes the mark: new plans take the account lock, a plan with
.B \-online
is refused. Runs that started finish; before a downgrade to a version
before 0.52.0 wait for them (or cancel the jobs, which unlocks their
mailboxes). Role:
.BR repair .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-limit " n"
Mailboxes per page (mailbox list), 1 to 1000; 100 by default.
.TP
.BI \-after " name"
Continue the listing after this mailbox name.
.TP
.B \-all
Fetch every page.
.TP
.BI \-special-use " list"
Special-use attributes of a new mailbox, comma-separated. A client finds
such a mailbox by its attribute rather than by its name, which is what
makes "Drafts" work in a client that calls it something else. The server
creates
.IR \eDrafts ", " \eSent ", " \eTrash ", " \eJunk " and " \eArchive
for every account on its own, which
.I server.autocreate
configures, so this flag is for the rest: RFC 6154 also defines
.IR \eAll " and " \eFlagged ,
RFC 8457 defines
.IR \eImportant .
The value is stored as given; the server does not check that no other
mailbox of the account carries the same attribute.
.TP
.BI \-if-match " etag"
Make the change conditional. The ETag is printed by every command that
changes a mailbox; if the mailbox changed meanwhile, the server refuses
with exit code 65 instead of overwriting someone else's change.
.TP
.BI \-pods " n"
.B mailbox lock enable:
the number of servers running now (required).
.TP
.B \-force
.B mailbox lock enable:
set the mark without the registrations check (audited).
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the answer as JSON.
.SH EXAMPLES
Public folders are administered like an account, with
.B ~public
as the account. They belong to nobody, so nobody has rights on them
until an entry grants some; see
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1).
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl mailbox create '~public' Announcements
shardpostctl acl set '~public' Announcements anyone lr
shardpostctl mailbox list -all '~public'
.RE
.fi
.PP
Which mailboxes does the account have, and how big is the INBOX?
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl mailbox list -all user@example.org
shardpostctl mailbox show user@example.org INBOX
.RE
.fi
.PP
Create an archive mailbox for a user who asked for one:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl mailbox create -special-use '\eArchive' \\
    user@example.org Archive
.RE
.fi
.PP
Rename a mailbox, but only if nobody else changed it since you looked:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl mailbox show user@example.org Projekte      # prints the etag
shardpostctl mailbox rename -if-match '"m-0808d3b73bb67293"' \\
    user@example.org Projekte Projects
.RE
.fi
.PP
Make a mailbox visible in the client:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl mailbox subscribe user@example.org Projects
.RE
.fi
.PP
Deleting a mailbox is not done here. It is a planned job, so you can see
what would be removed before anything happens; see
.BR shardpostctl-job (1):
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl plan -mailbox 'Archive/2019' mailbox_delete user@example.org
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 64
Wrong usage, or a mailbox name a new mailbox must not get.
.TP
.B 65
Conflict, or the ETag no longer matches.
.TP
.B 67
No such account or mailbox.
.TP
.B 73
The account is at its mailbox limit
.RB ( limits.mailboxes " or " limits.mailbox_names
in the server configuration; error
.BR limit_exceeded ).
.TP
.B 77
The caller lacks the role.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-acl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-job (1),
.BR shardpostctl-message (1)
